WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.160
VOA Special English Technology Report.

00:04.160 --> 00:12.120
Smartphones, tablet computers, and e-readers have become a part of everyday life for many

00:12.120 --> 00:13.560
people.

00:13.560 --> 00:20.460
They have also led to an explosion in the number of mobile applications.

00:20.460 --> 00:27.600
There is an app for just about anything you can think of, and more and more apps

00:27.600 --> 00:30.880
are being created every day.

00:30.880 --> 00:35.120
Robert Denazi is a Hollywood movie producer.

00:35.120 --> 00:44.080
He is perhaps best well known as the executive producer of Flightplan, the 2005 movie starring

00:44.080 --> 00:46.980
actress Jodi Foster.

00:46.980 --> 00:55.400
His most recent claim to fame is as co-developer of a new app called VoiceCal.

00:55.400 --> 01:02.000
It is a mobile calendar app that uses voice recognition software.

01:02.000 --> 01:08.560
Users are able to enter appointments into their iPhone calendar without having to type

01:08.560 --> 01:11.480
in the information.

01:11.480 --> 01:18.160
He said although he has a mobile phone, he had to stop what he was doing to put things

01:18.160 --> 01:20.320
in his calendar.

01:20.320 --> 01:27.920
In his words, it got to the point where I just wasn't using the calendar on my iPhone

01:27.920 --> 01:32.440
and neither was anybody else that I knew.

01:32.440 --> 01:37.560
Robert Denazi decided there had to be a better way.

01:37.560 --> 01:42.000
So he teamed up with a friend who is a developer.

01:42.000 --> 01:51.120
The two used voice recognition software from a company called Nuance to build VoiceCal.

01:51.120 --> 01:59.120
He says when you use VoiceCal, you can say any event you want to put into your calendar

01:59.120 --> 02:02.480
and it will appear there.

02:02.480 --> 02:09.760
If you have two or more events to add to the calendar, you separate them by using

02:09.760 --> 02:12.440
the word also.

02:12.440 --> 02:18.480
Mr. Denazi says all of this can be done hands-free.

02:18.480 --> 02:25.520
In settings, you can make it fully automatic, so I don't have to touch anything.

02:25.520 --> 02:34.120
He says Robert Denazi says there is a great need for an app like VoiceCal among busy

02:34.120 --> 02:37.320
businessmen like himself.

02:37.320 --> 02:42.680
He hopes the app will be successful over the long term.

02:42.680 --> 02:51.000
He says in the movie business, you can do all the advertising in the world and that can get

02:51.000 --> 02:58.320
you a great opening weekend, but if the movie is not good, it will die off.

02:58.320 --> 03:05.600
So really what supports a film just like what supports an app is word of mouth.

03:05.600 --> 03:15.600
Matt Kozlov is the head of a startup technology company called Moonshark in Los Angeles, California.

03:15.600 --> 03:19.360
The company makes game apps for mobile devices.

03:19.360 --> 03:25.000
It's an exploding marketplace right now, he says.

03:25.000 --> 03:32.240
There are hundreds of millions of devices out there right now that we can reach.

03:32.240 --> 03:40.520
Jennifer Lopez is helping Moonshark with a finger break dancing game called Dance Pad.

03:40.520 --> 03:48.000
The company is also working with film director John Woo on an action game expected to be

03:48.000 --> 03:51.880
released at the end of this year.

03:51.880 --> 03:55.560
For VOA Special English, I'm Alex Villareal.

